<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Warka Vase</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 3200–3000 BCE</span> · Also known as Uruk Vase</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Warka Vase is a carved alabaster vessel about one metre tall, found in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eanna">Eanna</a> precinct at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> (modern Warka) in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and dated to about 3200–3000 BCE. Its bands of relief show water, plants, animals, naked men carrying offerings and a procession to the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inanna">Inanna</a>, one of the earliest narrative works of art. Looted from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-museum-of-iraq">Iraq Museum</a> in 2003, it was returned damaged and restored.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/warka-vase.jpg" alt="Warka Vase" width="320" height="752" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Warka_vase_(background_retouched).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>German archaeologists found the vase as 15 fragments during their sixth excavation season at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>, in 1933 and 1934. The expedition's field book records it as find number W14873 under 2 January 1934, a large alabaster container about 96 cm high with flat reliefs. It lay in level III of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eanna">Eanna</a> district, of the Jemdat Nasr period, also called Uruk III, together with other objects, among them fragments of a second vase, which the excavators took for votive offerings. As it was not found in its original place, the level gives only its latest possible date.</p><p>The vase had already been broken and repaired in antiquity with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> bands. Because the reconstruction of its base is uncertain, its full height is estimated at 105 to 110 cm, of which the relief occupies 92 cm. The mouth measures 38 cm across, and the wall is 2 cm thick at the top and thickens towards the bottom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The reliefs run in three registers of 25, 17.5 and 20.5 cm from top to bottom; a band divides the lowest, so the vase is sometimes said to have four. At the bottom are the plants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> delta, natural reeds and cultivated grain such as date palm, barley and wheat, and above them rams and ewes walk in single file in strict profile. In the middle register nude men carry bowls and jars of fruit and grain.</p><p>The top register is a single scene at a temple. A female deity stands before two bundles of reeds and is offered a bowl of fruit and grain by a nude figure, while a ruler-figure in a ceremonial kilt and long belt faces her at the head of the procession. The scene may represent the ritual marriage of the goddess and her consort, which ensured the continued vitality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>A plaster cast of the vase stood for many decades in room 5 of the Vorderasiatisches Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. During the 2003 invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, in April, looters wrenched the original from its case in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-museum-of-iraq">National Museum of Iraq</a>, snapping it at the base. On 12 June 2003 three unidentified men brought it back to the museum during an amnesty, broken into 14 pieces. It was restored and is displayed in the Iraq Museum as number IM19606.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Warka Vase” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Warka Vase</b> and the Mask of Warka, two of Uruk’s greatest treasures, were looted from the Iraq Museum in Baghdad in 2003 and both were later recovered.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uruk">Uruk · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
